WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:05.040
From a pair of psychopathic dolls to a bunch of&nbsp;
friends erotically obsessed with car crashes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:08.760
there've been a lot of movies which&nbsp;
push the boundaries of the love scene.

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:14.160
According to University College London&nbsp;
researchers, there's an old folktale about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:19.920
women with vagina dentata, or a second set&nbsp;
of very sharp teeth down below. It's not a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:24.540
real medical condition, and probably stems from&nbsp;
men's fear and lack of understanding of female&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.540 --> 00:00:29.940
sexuality going back hundreds of years.&nbsp;
The 2007 low-budget horror comedy Teeth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.940 --> 00:00:35.100
took this old legend — and the sexual politics&nbsp;
that inspired it — as the basis for its story,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:39.180
creating an allegory about adolescence&nbsp;
and the physical changes that it brings.

00:00:39.180 --> 00:00:44.580
Jess Weixler's Dawn hasn't explored her&nbsp;
sexuality much, and is very involved in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.580 --> 00:00:49.800
her high school's chastity club. But that changes&nbsp;
when she meets Hale Appleman's Tobey. Things are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:54.180
going alright at first, until he suddenly&nbsp;
turns impatient and violent — turning a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.180 --> 00:00:58.980
love scene into a sickening assault scene.&nbsp;
But Dawn puts a swift end to it, thanks to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.980 --> 00:01:04.020
her previously undiscovered physical quirk,&nbsp;
effectively severing Tobey from his manhood.

00:01:04.740 --> 00:01:09.720
The Room — starring, written by, and&nbsp;
directed by Tommy Wiseau — is a cult classic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.720 --> 00:01:14.160
and widely considered among the worst movies&nbsp;
ever made. Amid the other atrocities in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:18.900
this movie is a pair of love scenes between&nbsp;
Wiseau's Johnny and his "future wife" Lisa,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.900 --> 00:01:24.000
played by Juliette Danielle. Well, it's really&nbsp;
just one love scene repeated twice — feeling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:28.020
like it's out of one of Cinemax's&nbsp;
low-budget erotica from the '80s and '90s.

00:01:28.020 --> 00:01:30.120
"Two's great, but three's a crowd."

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:36.120
"I get it. You guys wanna be alone."

00:01:36.120 --> 00:01:41.580
The scene is shot through gauzy white curtains —&nbsp;
and then a rainy window. The odd foreplay is lit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.580 --> 00:01:47.040
by candles and abrasive neon light, with Johnny&nbsp;
positively obsessed with a single red rose,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:52.200
plucking off each pedal and throwing them at&nbsp;
Lisa. But when we finally reach the big moment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:56.580
the couple are misaligned — seemingly&nbsp;
unable to understand what's supposed to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.580 --> 00:02:00.420
happen. And then it just keeps&nbsp;
going, and going, and going.

00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:06.660
David Cronenberg's films are always delightfully&nbsp;
provocative, transgressive, and bewildering. He's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.660 --> 00:02:12.720
famous for his body horror films and psychological&nbsp;
thrillers. In 1996 he wrote and directed Crash,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:18.720
an NC-17-rated adaptation of the dark and&nbsp;
edgy novel of the same name by J.G. Ballard.

00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:24.480
This movie is all about obscure, fetishistic&nbsp;
sex. James Spader plays James Ballard,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:30.120
who — after getting into a near-fatal car accident&nbsp;
— finds a group of people who derive pleasure from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.120 --> 00:02:36.180
vehicular mishaps. They channel the fear, anxiety,&nbsp;
and adrenaline rush into erotic energy — which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.180 --> 00:02:41.760
means that several of Crash's intimate scenes&nbsp;
are also violent car wrecks. And as you know, car&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:47.820
crashes cause injuries, and for Rosanna Arquette's&nbsp;
Gabrielle, it's a big wound on her leg. Spader's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.820 --> 00:02:53.100
character then treats it as he would any other&nbsp;
naturally occurring bodily orifice on a partner.

00:02:53.940 --> 00:03:00.600
Seth Rogen's done a lot of cringey sex scenes,&nbsp;
but Judd Apatow's 2007 coming-of-age comedy may&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.600 --> 00:03:06.360
just take the cake. Love scenes can trigger a lot&nbsp;
of unexpected or unintended reactions — laughter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.360 --> 00:03:12.120
disgust, confusion, and even annoyance. But&nbsp;
Apatow goes for the whole pot in Knocked Up.

00:03:12.120 --> 00:03:17.160
In the film, Rogen's Ben is an immature and&nbsp;
irresponsible guy forced to quickly become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.160 --> 00:03:22.440
an adult when Katherine Heigl's Alison learns&nbsp;
she's pregnant from their one-night stand. Alison&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.440 --> 00:03:27.120
decides to carry the child to term and requests&nbsp;
that Ben come back into her life as a partner.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.120 --> 00:03:32.220
They bicker throughout the movie, particularly&nbsp;
over whiny and lazy Ben's inability and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.220 --> 00:03:37.320
unwillingness to change. But they still decide to&nbsp;
make a go of a more permanent romantic situation.

00:03:37.320 --> 00:03:42.480
You'd think the initial clumsy, drunk lovemaking&nbsp;
scene between the two of them, setting the drama&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:47.520
in motion, is the most uncomfortable one in&nbsp;
the film. But no. After they're back together,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.520 --> 00:03:52.740
Ben and Alison find themselves in the middle&nbsp;
of another baby-making act, except Alison&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.740 --> 00:03:57.060
is already very far into her pregnancy.&nbsp;
Rather than be in the moment in any way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.060 --> 00:04:01.740
Ben obsesses over a myth that's dispelled&nbsp;
on day one of junior high health class.

00:04:02.070 --> 00:04:05.150
"All I see is our baby getting&nbsp;
poked in the face with my p---s."

00:04:05.150 --> 00:04:06.414
"Trust me, you're not even close."

